course: language
lesson: 39
title: Talking well: politeness, listening and saying sorry
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Talking well is not only about the right words; it's about how you treat the person in front of you: thanking, taking turns, listening and saying sorry properly.
explain: Gran hands Sam a present, Mum raises an eyebrow and says "What do you say?", and Sam says "Thanks, Gran"; tiny words like "please" and "thank you" make people feel noticed.
explain: Politeness sounds different in different places: a London shop says "Cheers, love", Manchester says "Ta, love", Glasgow says "Cheers, hen", and Alabama says "Yes, ma'am"; none of them is more correct, they are all kind.
explain: A real apology says what you did and how you'll put it right; "Sorry you're upset" is not the same as "Sorry I broke your toy".
explain: Good listening shows on your face: look at the person, let them finish, and ask a question back.
fact: "Please" and "thank you" are basic polite words in English; parents teach them early, and people notice when they are missing.
fact: "Cheers" and "ta" are casual British ways to say thanks; "love" is a friendly word from strangers in much of England, and in Glasgow and the west of Scotland "hen" is used the same way to women and girls.
fact: To ask someone to repeat, "Sorry?" and "Pardon?" are both polite in Britain today; "What?" can sound rude unless it is said softly; which word is best was once a famous argument in the 1950s.
fact: In the southern USA children often say "sir" and "ma'am" to adults; in British schools pupils often call teachers "Miss" or "Sir"; both show respect.
fact: Taking turns means letting each person speak; interrupting can seem rude, though in some families and cultures people talk over each other more; waiting for a pause shows respect.
fact: Starting with "No offence, but..." does not stop words from hurting; kind criticism is specific and helpful, like "I love the colours, maybe make the dog bigger?".
fact: A real apology says what you did and how you'll make it right: "I'm sorry I broke your toy, I'll help fix it."
fact: Ukrainians politely call a teacher by first name and patronymic, a name made from the father's first name: "Олена Петрівна" means Olena, daughter of Petro; English pupils say Mr, Mrs, Ms or Miss with the surname.
fact: In Chinese names the family name comes first: in "Wang Lei", Wang is the surname; some Chinese people swap the order in English-speaking countries, so it's polite to ask which name to use.
fact: In Australia "G'day" means hello and "How ya going?" means "How are you?"; the usual answer is "Good, thanks, and you?".
fact: Active listening means paying full attention and showing it: looking at the person, not checking your phone, and asking a question back.
exercise: Gran gives Sam a present and Mum says "What do you say?" What should Sam say? | Thanks, Gran | What is it? | Is that all? | I wanted a different one
exercise: A cashier in Manchester says "Ta, love." What does "ta" mean? | Thanks | Goodbye for now | Hurry up, please | Take your change
exercise: In Glasgow a shopkeeper says "Cheers, hen" to a girl. What is "hen" here? | A friendly word, like love or dear | A rude word for a noisy person | A joke about chickens | A word for someone very young
exercise: Ollie doesn't hear his teacher. Which is the most polite thing to say? | Sorry? | What? | Eh? | Huh?
exercise: A girl from Alabama says "Yes, ma'am" to her teacher. What is she showing? | Respect | Cheekiness towards the teacher | That she has forgotten the name | That she is cross
exercise: Dan keeps cutting into his friend's story. What should he do? | Wait for a pause and then add his bit | Talk louder so his own story wins | Leave the room until the story ends | Finish the story for his friend
exercise: Lily says "No offence, but your drawing is rubbish." Why is her friend hurt? | "No offence" doesn't stop unkind words from hurting | Her friend didn't hear the words "no offence" | Lily used too many long words in one go | Her friend thought Lily was talking about someone else
exercise: Which is a kind way to give criticism? | I love the colours; maybe the dog could be bigger? | No offence, but that's the worst dog ever drawn | It's fine, I suppose, whatever, I don't really care | Just start again, this one can't be saved
exercise: Leo breaks his sister's toy. Which is a real apology? | I'm sorry I broke your toy; I'll help fix it | Sorry you're upset about it, but that's life | Sorry, but it was already a bit wobbly anyway | It's only a toy, you've got loads of others
exercise: Ukrainian pupils call their teacher "Олена Петрівна". What is "Петрівна"? | A name made from her father's name | Her surname, the family name | Her middle name chosen by her parents | A Ukrainian word for "teacher"
exercise: How would English pupils usually address Mrs Kovalenko? | Mrs Kovalenko | Olena | Teacher Olena | Kovalenko
exercise: Ava meets "Wang Lei". Which is the family name? | Wang | Lei | Both together | Neither, it's a nickname
exercise: An old man in Sydney says "How ya going?" What does he mean? | How are you? | Where are you going today? | How are you travelling, by bus? | Are you going away?
exercise: What's the usual answer to "How ya going?" in Australia? | Good, thanks, and you? | I'm going to school on the bus | I'm going by foot, mate | Nowhere, I'm staying here
exercise: Kai's friend tells him about her sick dog. What shows good listening? | Looking at her and asking "Is she going to be OK?" | Nodding while scrolling through videos on his phone | Telling her about his own dog straight away | Saying "Dogs get sick all the time, don't worry"
exercise: What is "G'day"? | An Australian hello | An Australian goodbye | A rude word in Australia | A day off school
test: Your mate goes "polite people say pardon, saying sorry when you didn't hear is wrong, innit." What's true? | Both "sorry?" and "pardon?" are polite today | He's right, only "pardon?" is correct English | "What?" is the only correct choice in Britain | You should say nothing and just guess
test: Mia from London answers "I'm going to school" when a man in Sydney says "How ya going?". What happened? | She thought he asked where; he asked how she is | He was asking her where she was going | He wanted to know if she was walking or on the bus | She answered perfectly the Australian way
test: A shopkeeper in Leeds says "There you go, love." Your cousin from abroad thinks it's strange. What's true? | It's normal friendly politeness from strangers in much of England | The shopkeeper must know your family well | It means the shopkeeper is in love with you | It's a rude thing to say to a customer
test: What would happen if Leo kept saying "Sorry you're upset" every time he broke something? | His sister would feel he never takes responsibility | His sister would feel fully apologised to every time | His sister would start breaking his toys to be fair | Nothing, because all sorrys are exactly the same
test: Your brother texts his mate the whole time Gran is telling a story. What would a better listener do? | Put the phone down, look at Gran and ask about the story | Keep texting but say "uh-huh" every few seconds | Interrupt Gran to tell her about the texts | Leave the room to text in peace and come back
test: A Gen Z cousin says "no offence but your fit is mid." ("Fit" means outfit; "mid" means not great.) What's true? | "No offence" doesn't make it less unkind | "No offence" means it can't possibly hurt | It's a big compliment in Gen Z slang | "Mid" means the outfit is the best one there
test: Your new classmate is called "Zhang Wei". You're not sure what to call her. What's the polite move? | Ask her which name she likes to be called | Just call her "Zhang" because it comes first | Make up an English nickname for her | Avoid calling her anything at all
test: A boy from Alabama says "Yes, sir" to your dad. Your mate says "that's fake posh." What's true? | It's a normal sign of respect in the southern USA | He's showing off to look clever | It's rude, because "sir" is for kings only | It's only said in old films
test: Your Glaswegian mate's mum says "Cheers, hen, ye're a wee darlin'." ("Wee" means little.) Is she being rude? | No, it's warm, friendly Glasgow talk | Yes, "hen" is a rude word in Scotland | Yes, she's calling you a chicken | No, but she thinks you are a baby
test: Two friends keep talking over each other at dinner, and both seem happy. What's fair? | In some families people overlap more; it's not always rude | It's always rude, and someone should stop them | It means they are having a big argument | They must not be friends any more
test: Your teacher, Mr Okafor, asks you to say thanks to the visitor. Which is best? | Thank you for coming to talk to us | Cheers for that, mate, ta-ra | Thanks I guess, it was OK | You can go now, thanks
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a genuine apology? | An acknowledgement of one's action together with an offer to make amends | An expression of regret at another person's feelings without admission of fault | A statement that the injured party ought not to be upset | A brief remark that the damage was of no consequence
exam: Identify the meaning of the Australian enquiry "How ya going?". | An enquiry after the wellbeing of the person addressed | An enquiry as to the destination of the person addressed | An enquiry as to the mode of transport employed | An instruction to depart without delay
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Ukrainian form of address "Олена Петрівна". | It comprises a given name and a patronymic derived from the father's given name | It comprises a given name and the family surname | It comprises two given names chosen by the parents | It comprises a title meaning "teacher" and a surname
exam: In respect of a Chinese name such as "Wang Lei", ascertain the position of the family name. | First | Last | Middle | Absent
